# Exam track — Theme: Describing a Picture — Sub-lesson 1: General Description
#
# The fifth picture theme, and the first PUBLIC-TRANSPORT scene: a bus stop on a
# city street, overcast daytime, several people waiting under a glass shelter
# while a line-9 bus pulls up. Where the other themes are a market, a funfair, a
# living room and a pedestrian street, this one adds routine commuting as a WOU +
# WAT scene, and its own WÉINI angle in 03: the trees are bare (like groussgaass's
# winter photo) but the people are in noticeably lighter clothing — evidence that
# points two different ways at once, which is new relative to the earlier themes'
# single-direction evidence.
#
# The photo: a bus shelter on a city sidewalk, a green advertisement poster and a
# street lamp beside it, bare trees either side, tall pastel-coloured buildings
# behind, a couple of parked cars on the left, and on the right a blue-white-orange
# city bus pulling up to the stop. Several people wait at the shelter; a man with a
# backpack stands near the front on the sidewalk.
#
# The photo's coverage is split across three sub-lessons, one per exam task:
#   01 — WOU + WAT: naming the place, the objects and their positions, and what
#        the people are doing.                              ← this file
#   02 — WIEN: the people, 1–2 of them described visually.
#   03 — WÉINI: the time of day and a hedged guess at the season, weighing
#        contradictory evidence.
#
# PURE DESCRIPTION. No @question examiner prompts and no first-person opinion,
# preference or feeling — those belong to the topic themes. A depicted person's
# visible state is fine: that is observation. Rule of thumb — if a stranger could
# not check the sentence against the photo, it does not belong here.
#
# Not authored, for failing exactly that test: the shelter's own signage
# ("Badanstalt"), the poster's brand text ("BFRESH"), and the bus's route number
# and destination display. Brand text and route numbers are neither Luxembourgish
# vocabulary nor checkable description of the *scene*, so the bus ships here as
# the plain `de Bus`.
#
# Vocabulary and grammar are held to A1–A2 deliberately, same as the other
# picture themes: present tense, simple main clauses, spatial adverbs +
# prepositions, no attributive adjectives on masculine or neuter nouns.
#
# This file NAMES THE PLACE, which is the examiner's first question:
# de Busarrêt. LOD lists both `Busarrêt` (m) and `Bushaltestell` (f) as plain
# dictionary words for "bus stop" — `Busarrêt` is the shorter, more colloquial
# term and is used throughout this theme.
#
# Nouns are taught in the number the content actually uses (d'Beem, d'Autoen,
# d'Gebaier as plurals only — no unused singular) — an unused singular is a free
# Element standing between the learner and the 100% gate.
#
# Ten @fill blocks, matching the theme's answering-pattern density: the existential
# "vill lass" idiom, the background/foreground pair, the additive `Ausserdeem`
# listing, the exemplifying `Zum Beispill` listing, the coordinated-inventory
# frame, the place-noun frame, the what-are-they-doing verb frame, and one row
# each for `wann` (inversion) and `well` (justification) — most reused shapes from
# the library, each given a pairing no earlier picture theme has taught. See the
# skill's references/content-contract.md for the mechanized bounds.

@lesson P4.01 "Busarrêt: General Description"

# Pre-cropped to 16:9 and encoded as WebP at 880px — see
# .claude/memory/picture-description-theme.md for the pipeline. @image-alt stays:
# it is the a11y text, and the caption of the placeholder frame if the file is
# ever missing.
@image "/assets/exam/picture/busarret/img/busarret.webp"
@image-alt "A bus stop on a city street: several people wait under a glass shelter next to a green advertisement poster and a street lamp, bare trees either side, tall pastel buildings behind, parked cars on the left, and a blue-white-orange city bus pulling up on the right."

# --- Talking about a picture ---
@word d'Bild = the picture
@word gesinn = to see

# --- Naming the place ---
@word de Busarrêt = the bus stop
@word de Bus = the bus
@word d'Strooss = the street
@word d'Stad = the city

# --- What is at the stop ---
@word d'Gebaier = the buildings
@word d'Beem = the trees
@word d'Autoen = the cars
@word d'Stroosseluucht = the street lamp
@word d'Reklamm = the advertisement
@word de Rucksak = the backpack

# --- Positions ---
@word lénks = on the left
@word riets = on the right
@word hannert = behind
@word de Vierdergrond = the foreground
@word den Hannergrond = the background

# --- People and what they do ---
@word d'Leit = the people
@word waarden = to wait
@word ukommen = to arrive

@sentence
@lu Dëst ass e Bild vun engem Busarrêt.
@en This is a picture of a bus stop.
@distractor-lu Buttek
@distractor-lu Schoul
@distractor-en shop
@distractor-en school

@sentence
@lu Vill Leit waarden op de Bus.
@en A lot of people are waiting for the bus.
@distractor-lu kee
@distractor-lu Auto
@distractor-en no
@distractor-en car

@sentence
@lu Lénks vum Busarrêt stinn Autoen.
@en On the left of the bus stop there are cars.
@distractor-lu riets
@distractor-lu Beem
@distractor-en right
@distractor-en trees

@sentence
@lu Hannert dem Busarrêt stinn héich Gebaier.
@en Behind the bus stop there are tall buildings.
@distractor-lu virun
@distractor-lu Autoen
@distractor-en in front
@distractor-en cars

@sentence
@lu Nieft dem Busarrêt steet eng Stroosseluucht.
@en Next to the bus stop there is a street lamp.
@distractor-lu Bam
@distractor-lu Reklamm
@distractor-en tree
@distractor-en advertisement

@sentence
@lu Riets kënnt de Bus un.
@en On the right the bus is arriving.
@distractor-lu lénks
@distractor-lu Auto
@distractor-en left
@distractor-en car

@sentence
@lu De Mann ass jonk an huet e Rucksak.
@en The man is young and has a backpack.
@distractor-lu al
@distractor-lu Kap
@distractor-en old
@distractor-en cap

# --- Reusable frames (@fill) ---

# The existential idiom Schueberfouer/chreschtmaart used for a busy scene, reused
# here for a bus stop — "vill lass" transfers to any scene with activity, which is
# what makes it a frame rather than a sentence about this photo.
@fill
@lu Um Busarrêt ass vill [lass].
@en At the bus stop a lot is [happening].
@distractor-lu roueg
@distractor-lu zou
@distractor-en quiet
@distractor-en closed

# The background inventory, reused with a new scene noun. `héich` stays fixed
# before the blank, so the plural noun is the answer and singular distractors
# fail on agreement (R3) as well as on the photo.
@fill
@lu Am Hannergrond ginn et héich [Gebaier].
@en In the background there are tall [buildings].
@distractor-lu Bam
@distractor-lu Bus
@distractor-en tree
@distractor-en bus

# The foreground inventory, mirrored from the background one above with a
# different position word and noun. `d'` stays fixed, ruling out masculine tiles
# on grammar (R4) as well as on the photo.
@fill
@lu Am Vierdergrond gesinn ech d'[Leit].
@en In the foreground I see the [people].
@distractor-lu Bus
@distractor-lu Bam
@distractor-en bus
@distractor-en tree

# The additive-listing frame, reused for the stop's cars and advertisement.
# `Ausserdeem` forces inversion (`gesinn ech`), an order the learner reads rather
# than assembles. Two blanks of different slot (bare plural noun, then singular
# after the fixed `d'`), so they cannot swap (R6); `d'` also keeps the second
# blank clear of the Eifeler Regel (R5).
@fill
@lu Ausserdeem gesinn ech [Autoen] an d'[Reklamm].
@en Besides I see [cars] and the [advertisement].
@distractor-lu Gebaier
@distractor-lu Bam
@distractor-en buildings
@distractor-en tree

# The exemplifying-listing frame — `e puer` forces a plural, so the singular
# distractors fail on agreement as well as on the photo.
@fill
@lu Zum Beispill gesinn ech e puer [Beem].
@en For example I see a few [trees].
@distractor-lu Bus
@distractor-lu Reklamm
@distractor-en bus
@distractor-en advertisement

# A single-noun coordinated-inventory frame — bare trees stand on both sides of
# the street, so `lénks a riets` stays fixed and the blank is the one scene noun,
# with `vill` forcing a plural. Both distractors are single, one-sided items
# (one bus, one poster), so neither is true of "on the left and right".
@fill
@lu Lénks a riets ginn et vill [Beem].
@en On the left and right there are many [trees].
@distractor-lu Bus
@distractor-lu Reklamm
@distractor-en bus
@distractor-en advertisement

# The place-noun frame from groussgaass/chreschtmaart, reused for a street rather
# than a square. `enger` stays fixed and feminine, ruling out masculine `Bus` and
# neuter `Gebai` on grammar alone (R4) as well as on the photo.
@fill
@lu D'Leit op dem Bild sinn an enger [Strooss].
@en The people in the picture are in a [street].
@distractor-lu Bus
@distractor-lu Gebai
@distractor-en bus
@distractor-en building

# The what-are-they-doing verb frame. The blank is the verb, and the plural
# subject `d'Leit` makes the singular form the sharpest distractor — a noun
# cannot occupy a verb slot at all (R3).
@fill
@lu Um Busarrêt [waarden] d'Leit op de Bus.
@en At the bus stop the people [are waiting] for the bus.
@distractor-lu waart
@distractor-lu Autoen
@distractor-en waits
@distractor-en cars

# `wann` — the leading clause forces inversion in the main clause, an order the
# learner reads rather than assembles. The blank is the inverted verb; the two
# distractors are other verbs that do not fit a bus arriving.
@fill
@lu Wann vill Leit waarden, [kënnt] de Bus séier.
@en When a lot of people are waiting, the bus [comes] quickly.
@distractor-lu geet
@distractor-lu bleift
@distractor-en goes
@distractor-en stays

# `well` outside the hedge register — reasoning about the scene, not a weather
# guess. The comma both ends the clause and exempts the blank from the Eifeler
# Regel.
@fill
@lu De Busarrêt gesäit [voll] aus, well vill Leit do sinn.
@en The bus stop looks [full], because a lot of people are there.
@distractor-lu eidel
@distractor-lu laang
@distractor-en empty
@distractor-en long
